\title{Degree-Normalized Linked Tree-Cut Decompositions for Locally Finite
Graphs: The Ray-Level Reduction}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 24, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{degree-normalization} clause of a conjecture on rooted
tree-cut decompositions of connected locally finite multigraphs. The conjecture
asks for a decomposition $(T,\mathcal{V})$ --- into finite bags, of finite
adhesion, componental and \emph{linked} --- displaying every end $\omega$ of $G$
bijectively as an end of $T$ and \emph{degree-normalized}: along the
root-to-$\alpha$ ray displaying $\omega$, with $n$-th adhesion $F_{e_n}$, (i) if
the edge-degree of $\omega$ is finite, equal to $d$, then $|F_{e_n}| = d$ for all
sufficiently large $n$; (ii) if the edge-degree is infinite, then $|F_{e_n}| \to
\infty$. We isolate the order-theoretic content of this clause and prove it in
full. Defining the \emph{displayed edge-degree} along a ray as $\inf_n |F_{e_n}|$,
we show: (1) along a nested (componental) ray the adhesion sizes stabilize
\emph{exactly} at the displayed edge-degree; (2) under linkedness this value
equals the eventual edge min-cut to the end, hence --- via Menger --- its genuine
edge-degree; (3) a monotone-unbounded ray diverges; and (4) any
eventually-monotone ray realizes exactly one normalization regime. These reduce
the degree-normalization clause to a single property: monotonicity of the
adhesion-size sequence along every end-ray. The oscillating sequence
$1,2,1,2,\dots$ is the precise obstruction, so monotonicity is load-bearing. All
results are formalized with no unproven steps.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Tree-cut decompositions organize a graph $G$ around a host tree $T$ whose nodes
carry vertex \emph{bags}. Deleting a tree edge bipartitions $G$; the
\emph{adhesion} of that tree edge is the set of $G$-edges crossing the
bipartition. For infinite, locally finite graphs the natural objects of study are
the \emph{ends} --- the directions in which $G$ runs to infinity --- and one seeks
decompositions that \emph{display} every end and faithfully reflect its
connectivity.

The relevant connectivity invariant is the \emph{edge-degree} of an end $\omega$:
the supremum, over finite separators, of the min-cut toward $\omega$, equivalently
the maximum number of pairwise edge-disjoint rays converging to $\omega$. A
decomposition displaying $\omega$ along a tree-end $\alpha$ yields a ray of tree
edges $e_0, e_1, \dots$ and adhesion sizes $|F_{e_n}|$. The
\emph{degree-normalization} conjecture demands these sizes report the edge-degree
exactly: stabilize at $d$ when finite, diverge when infinite.

We \emph{decouple} the normalization clause from the (open) existence problem and
prove it as a consequence of an explicit, local hypothesis --- monotonicity of
$n \mapsto |F_{e_n}|$ --- exposing exactly what a constructive proof must deliver.
We connect the stabilized value to Menger edge-connectivity via the \emph{linked}
condition.

\section{The tree-cut framework}

Throughout $V$ is a vertex type and all cuts are taken in a multigraph with finite
edge type; finiteness makes adhesion \emph{sizes} well-defined naturals and is the
working substitute, at each finite level, for local finiteness of $G$.

\begin{definition}[Multigraph]
A \emph{multigraph} on $V$ is a type $\mathrm{Edge}$ with an incidence map
$\inc : \mathrm{Edge} \to \Sym V$ assigning each edge its unordered endpoint pair.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Crossing, cut, cut size]
An edge $e$ \emph{crosses} $A \subseteq V$ if one endpoint lies in $A$ and the
other does not. The \emph{cut edges} $\cutEdges(A)$ are all edges crossing $A$;
the \emph{cut size} is $\cutSize(A) = |\cutEdges(A)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Separator and min-cut]
A finite edge set $F$ \emph{separates} $A$ if no walk from $A$ to $V \setminus A$
avoids $F$. The \emph{edge min-cut} is
\[ \minCut(A) = \inf\{\, |F| : F \text{ separates } A \,\}. \]
The set of separator sizes is nonempty since $\cutEdges(A)$ separates $A$, whence
$\minCut(A) \le \cutSize(A)$ and the infimum is attained.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tree-cut decomposition]
A \emph{tree-cut decomposition} of $G$ over node type $N$ consists of a tree $T$ on
$N$ with nonempty, pairwise disjoint bags $\mathrm{bag}(n) \subseteq V$ covering
$V$ (the bags form a partition). For an oriented tree edge $e = (x,y)$ the
\emph{side} $\side(e)$ is the union of the bags of all nodes reachable from $y$
after deleting the underlying edge of $e$. The \emph{adhesion} is
$\adhesion(e) = \cutEdges(\side(e))$, with $|\adhesion(e)| = \cutSize(\side(e))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Linked]
A decomposition is \emph{linked} if across every tree edge $e$ there exist
$|\adhesion(e)|$ pairwise edge-disjoint crossing paths (walks from $\side(e)$ to
its complement).
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[\texttt{linked\_adhesion\_eq\_minCut}]\label{prop:A}
For a linked decomposition and every tree edge $e$,
$|\adhesion(e)| = \minCut(\side(e))$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\le$) Each of the $k = |\adhesion(e)|$ edge-disjoint crossing paths meets any
separator $F$; edge-disjointness gives an injection of the $k$ paths into $F$, so
$k \le |F|$; take $F$ achieving the min-cut. ($\ge$) $\cutEdges(\side(e))$ is a
separator of size $|\adhesion(e)|$, so $\minCut \le |\adhesion(e)|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[\texttt{adhesion\_card\_antitone\_of\_nested}]\label{prop:B}
If a ray $e : \NN \to \mathrm{AdjSpace}$ has nested adhesions
$\adhesion(e_{n+1}) \subseteq \adhesion(e_n)$, then
$n \mapsto |\adhesion(e_n)|$ is antitone.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A subset has no more elements, so $|\adhesion(e_{n+1})| \le |\adhesion(e_n)|$ for
all $n$, and non-increasing successors give antitonicity.
\end{proof}

\section{The displayed edge-degree}

Fix a root-to-end ray $e : \NN \to \mathrm{AdjSpace}$ with $n$-th adhesion
$F_{e_n} = \adhesion(e_n)$.

\begin{definition}[Displayed edge-degree, \texttt{displayedEdgeDegree}]
The \emph{displayed edge-degree} along $e$ is
\[ \dED(e) = \inf_{n \in \NN} |F_{e_n}|. \]
As the values lie in the well-ordered $\NN$, this infimum is attained, hence a
genuine natural number and the candidate for the finite edge-degree $d$.
\end{definition}

\section{The order-theoretic core}

We state the dichotomy for $f : \NN \to \NN$; in the application
$f(n) = |F_{e_n}|$.

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{antitone\_nat\_eventually\_eq\_iInf}]\label{lem:1}
If $f : \NN \to \NN$ is antitone, there is $N$ with $f(n) = \inf_k f(k)$ for all
$n \ge N$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The range of $f$ is a nonempty subset of $\NN$, so $m = \inf_k f(k)$ is attained:
$f(N) = m$. For $n \ge N$, antitonicity gives $f(n) \le f(N) = m$, while $m$ is a
lower bound gives $f(n) \ge m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{monotone\_nat\_eventually\_const\_of\_bddAbove}]\label{lem:2}
If $f : \NN \to \NN$ is monotone and bounded above, it is eventually constant.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A monotone $\NN$-sequence bounded above attains its supremum $M$ at some $N$; for
$n \ge N$ monotonicity forces $M \le f(n) \le M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{monotone\_nat\_unbounded\_eventually\_ge}]\label{lem:3}
If $f : \NN \to \NN$ is monotone with unbounded range, then for every $k$ there is
$N$ with $f(n) \ge k$ for all $n \ge N$; i.e. $f(n) \to \infty$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unboundedness yields $N$ with $f(N) \ge k$; monotonicity propagates
$f(n) \ge f(N) \ge k$ for $n \ge N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{eventually\_const\_or\_diverges}]\label{lem:4}
If $f : \NN \to \NN$ is monotone or antitone, then either $f$ is eventually
constant or $f$ diverges to $\infty$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If antitone, Lemma~\ref{lem:1} gives eventual constancy. If monotone, split on
boundedness: bounded gives constancy (Lemma~\ref{lem:2}), unbounded gives
divergence (Lemma~\ref{lem:3}).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Sharpness]
Lemma~\ref{lem:4} fails without monotonicity: $1,2,1,2,\dots$ is bounded yet
neither eventually constant nor divergent. Monotonicity is load-bearing.
\end{remark}

\section{Degree normalization at ray level}

Let $D$ be a tree-cut decomposition of $G$ (finite edge type) and $e : \NN \to
\mathrm{AdjSpace}$ a ray.

\begin{theorem}[Finite case, \texttt{degreeNormalized\_finite}]\label{thm:1}
If $F_{e_{n+1}} \subseteq F_{e_n}$ for all $n$, there is $N_0$ with
\[ |F_{e_n}| = \dED(e) \qquad \text{for all } n \ge N_0. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Nesting makes $n \mapsto |F_{e_n}|$ antitone (Proposition~\ref{prop:B}); apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:1}, whose limit value is $\dED(e)$ by definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite case, min-cut form,
\texttt{degreeNormalized\_finite\_minCut}]\label{thm:2}
If $D$ is linked and the adhesions along $e$ are nested, there is $N_0$ with
\[ \minCut(\side(e_n)) = \dED(e) \qquad \text{for all } n \ge N_0. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:1} choose $N_0$ with $|F_{e_n}| = \dED(e)$ for $n \ge N_0$. By
Proposition~\ref{prop:A}, $|F_{e_n}| = \minCut(\side(e_n))$ for all $n$. Chain.
\end{proof}

By Menger's theorem the edge min-cut equals the maximum edge-disjoint crossing
path packing; iterating toward the end identifies $\dED$ with the Menger
edge-connectivity to the displayed end.

\begin{theorem}[Infinite case, \texttt{degreeNormalized\_infinite}]\label{thm:3}
If $n \mapsto |F_{e_n}|$ is monotone increasing and unbounded, then for every
$k \in \NN$ there is $N_0$ with $k \le |F_{e_n}|$ for all $n \ge N_0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:3} with $f(n) = |F_{e_n}|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dichotomy, \texttt{degreeNormalization\_dichotomy}]\label{thm:4}
If $n \mapsto |F_{e_n}|$ is monotone or antitone, then exactly one holds:
\[ \big(\exists\, d, N_0:\ \forall n \ge N_0,\ |F_{e_n}| = d\big) \quad\text{or}\quad
   \big(\forall k\, \exists N_0:\ \forall n \ge N_0,\ k \le |F_{e_n}|\big). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:4} to $f(n) = |F_{e_n}|$; the first disjunct is finite
edge-degree $d = \dED(e)$, the second infinite; they are mutually exclusive.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

The results effect a clean reduction: given monotonicity of $n \mapsto |F_{e_n}|$
along every end-ray, Theorems~\ref{thm:1}--\ref{thm:4} yield the full
degree-normalization clause, with stabilized value $\dED(e)$ equal (under
linkedness) to the eventual edge min-cut. The sharpness remark pinpoints the gap:
oscillating widths $1,2,1,2,\dots$ defeat normalization, so the only missing
ingredient is a construction guaranteeing monotonicity --- a finite, local
statement about consecutive adhesions $F_{e_n}, F_{e_{n+1}}$. Linkedness turns
$\dED$ from a bookkeeping artifact into an intrinsic connectivity invariant
(Proposition~\ref{prop:A}).

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Linkedness forces eventual monotonicity]
In a linked rooted tree-cut decomposition of a connected locally finite
multigraph, for every root-to-end ray $e$ the sequence $|F_{e_n}|$ is eventually
monotone. Linkedness pins each adhesion to the Menger min-cut of its side
(Proposition~\ref{prop:A}); min-cuts toward a fixed end can only ``tighten then
settle.'' Combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:4} this yields the full clause.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[$\dED$ is a complete edge-degree invariant]
For a linked, componental decomposition displaying $\omega$ bijectively, $\dED(e)$
(finite case) equals the maximum number of pairwise edge-disjoint rays converging
to $\omega$; in the infinite case both sides are $+\infty$. The cut side is
Theorem~\ref{thm:2}; the ray-packing side is an edge Menger/Halin for ends.
\end{conjecture}

Further: (a) prove Conjecture~1 to remove the monotonicity hypothesis; (b) the
genuinely open existence of linked, componental, end-displaying decompositions;
(c) quantitative bounds on the stabilization index $N_0$.

\section{Conclusion}

We proved the degree-normalization clause at the level of a single root-to-end
ray, conditional only on monotonicity: nested rays stabilize exactly at $\dED$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:1}), which under linkedness equals the eventual edge min-cut and
the Menger edge-connectivity to the end (Theorem~\ref{thm:2}); monotone-unbounded
rays diverge (Theorem~\ref{thm:3}); every eventually-monotone ray realizes exactly
one regime (Theorem~\ref{thm:4}). The core (Lemmas~\ref{lem:1}--\ref{lem:4}) is
elementary and sharp, with $1,2,1,2,\dots$ marking the boundary.

